

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Answered: a few similar forms (signup, profile edit), required-field checks plus a couple of format rules.

  Next fork — and this one has consequences, so here's the comparison before I ask.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much of the form does the shared module own. Validation alone is a pure data-in/errors-out function;
  form binding additionally owns reading inputs from the DOM, intercepting submit, and rendering errors.

  - [1mValidation only[0m — smallest interface, trivially testable with no DOM, and each form keeps its own submit handler.
    Costs you repeated boilerplate in every form: query the inputs, build the data object, loop the errors, put them on
    screen. Cheap to add binding on top later.
  - [1mValidation + form binding[0m — one call per form ([38;5;153mbindForm('#signup-form', rules, onSubmit)[39m) and no per-form
    boilerplate. Costs you a DOM-coupled module that needs a DOM to test, and it bakes in an error-display convention
    every form must accept. Harder to back out of once three forms depend on it.
  - [1mValidation + a thin error-rendering helper, binding left to each form[0m — middle ground: shared rules and a shared
    "show these errors" function, but each form still writes its own submit handler.

  Cheap to change later: adding binding on top of a pure validator. Expensive to change later: the rule-definition
  format itself, since every form's rules get rewritten if it changes.

  [1mMy recommendation: validation only.[0m With three similar forms the boilerplate is a few lines each, and it keeps the
  module DOM-free and testable in a repo that currently has no test setup at all. Binding can follow once you've seen
  the actual duplication.
